  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nMainly high clouds are spreading across much of the area today as\nan upper shortwave tracks from the Red River Valley toward the \nnorthern Gulf. Radar indicates light to moderate echoes aloft, but\nthere is a pronounced dry layer in the low levels with surface \ndewpoint depressions upwards of 20-25F which will inhibit most of \nthis precip from reaching the surface. Through tonight, very light\nrainfall is possible across northeast LA and south MS. Elsewhere,\nit will be scattered sprinkles at best, and most areas will \nreceive no rain at all. A surface ridge will build back into the \nregion on Monday as this system shifts south of our area.\n\nSurface ridging will remain anchored over the eastern CONUS \nthrough the upcoming week, with seasonably cool mornings and \nseasonably warm days with broad diurnal temp ranges owing to the \ndry airmass. Temps will steadily moderate by the end of the week. \nNo rainfall is expected through the work week. A late week system \nwill track from the Plains toward the Midwest, teasing potential \nfor rain in our area this weekend or early next week, but it \nremains unclear whether the associated front will even make it \ninto our area. Even with that system, the probability for \nsubstantial rainfall remains low at this time. /DL/|",
